Well, it's more complex than that, intensity from
black to white goes to the screen at a much higher
bandwidth, so you'll get a lot of juice out of the
fact that you can cut the pixels around and internal
to an ASCII character from "full on" to "full off"
completely effectively at the pixel by pixel level.

The limited color bandwidth problem only comes in at
the level where colors change, which for ASCII
NetHack is character width to character width, not
pixel width to pixel, and that's easily achieved
with the current color bandwidth.

At worst, colors might vary smoothly a bit across a
character, having the "correct value" only in the
center.

When my Amiga's monitor went south long after
Commodore went bankrupt, I used my Apple ][+ color
monitor with perfect success hooked to the Amiga's
NTSC output port to edit text, although it would
only present 40 characters in a screenwidth
completely readably, so I wrote really skinny code
and postings for a while.

